<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" title="In A.3 Qualitative Observations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Scale Can’t Overcome Pragmatics: The Impact of Reporting Bias on Vision-Language Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">CLIP ignores negations.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" title="In A.3 Qualitative Observations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Scale Can’t Overcome Pragmatics: The Impact of Reporting Bias on Vision-Language Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Models can count to smaller numbers better.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" title="In A.3 Qualitative Observations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Scale Can’t Overcome Pragmatics: The Impact of Reporting Bias on Vision-Language Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">“Left” and “right” are the most difficult spatial relations for VLMs.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4" title="In A.3 Qualitative Observations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Scale Can’t Overcome Pragmatics: The Impact of Reporting Bias on Vision-Language Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Contrastive VLMs can ignore keywords even when they do occur in the training data.</span></a></li>
</ol></li>
